  <article class="essay">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Lagom</p>
      <h1>Two shapes of intake. One of them runs.</h1>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Batch APIs and continuous events are different problems. The decision is dlt for a batch
          API, and Redpanda Connect for events. NATS waits for a second consumer of the same stream,
          or a need to replay. A source that is polled often can be written straight through, and
          NATS would add nothing. The event path is not in this project yet.
        </p>
        <p>
          dlt is a library, not a server. The job starts, pages the feed, keeps a cursor, writes,
          and exits. Between runs it holds no memory. The first source is the USGS earthquake feed:
          public, no key, a stable shape, a small volume. The work is the conveyor.
        </p>
        <p>
          The scheduled job asks dlt for the DuckLake destination. The accepted storage decision
          still says Postgres holds every layer until the spike passes. Both sentences are in the
          sources. The measured loads are on the USGS showcase, not repeated here as if the
          decision were closed. How many files the lake holds, and how many bytes, are not in those
          sources.
        </p>
      </div>
